+### Creating a JSON Patch
+
+```ts
+import { createPatch } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+createPatch(
+  { title: "Hello", tags: ["a", "b", "c"], meta: { rev: 1 } },
+  { title: "Hello", tags: ["a", "c"], meta: { rev: 2 } },
+); // [{ op: "remove", path: "/tags/1" }, { op: "replace", path: "/meta/rev", value: 2 }]
+```
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` returns a JSON Patch that turns `from` into `to` when applied with `applyPatch`.
+It describes the differences only: equal documents give `[]`, objects are compared member by member
+(regardless of member order), and arrays are aligned on their longest common subsequence of elements, so
+inserting or removing one element gives one operation and a changed element is diffed in place. A value
+whose type changes is replaced as a whole. Only `add`, `remove` and `replace` are used; the result of
+applying the patch equals `to`, though its members may be in another order.
+
+`from` and `to` must be JSON values, as for `applyPatch`; neither is modified, and the patch shares no
+object or array with them. It throws a `JsonPatchError` (with `index` `undefined`) when either is not a
+JSON value. When the differing middle parts of two arrays (once their common start and end are set
+aside) exceed a million pairs of elements, they are compared position by position instead: the patch is
+still correct, but may be longer.
